      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="77" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Suppose that $$f(1) = 2$$ , $$f(4) = 7$$ , $$f^{\prime}(1) = 5$$ , $$f^{\prime}(4) = 3$$ , and $$f^{''}$$ is continuous. Find the value of $$\int_{1}^{4}{xf^{''}(x)dx}$$ .</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$2$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="78" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Use integration by parts to show that $${\int f}(x)dx = xf(x) - {\int x}f^{\prime}(x)dx$$</item>
          <item>If $$f$$ and $$g$$ are inverse functions and $$f^{\prime}$$ is continuous, prove that $$\int_{a}^{b}{f(x)dx = bf(b) - af(a) - {\int_{f(a)}^{f(b)}{g(y)dy}}}$$ [Hint: Use part (a) and make the substitution $$y = f(x)$$ .]</item>
          <item>In the case where $$f$$ and $$g$$ are positive functions and $$b &gt; a &gt; 0$$ , draw a diagram to give a geometric interpretation of part (b).</item>
          <item>Use part (b) to evaluate $$\int_{1}^{e}{\ln xdx}$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="79" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Recall that the formula for integration by parts is obtained from the Product Rule. Use similar reasoning to obtain the following integration formula from the Quotient Rule. $$\int\frac{u}{v^{2}}\mathit{dv} = - \frac{u}{v} + \int\frac{1}{v}\mathit{du}$$</item>
          <item>Use the formula in part (a) to evaluate $$\int\frac{\ln x}{x^{2}}\mathit{dx}$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$- \frac{\ln x}{x} - \frac{1}{x} + C$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="80" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>The Wallis Product Formula for $$\pi$$ Let $$I_{n} = {\int_{0}^{\pi \slash 2}{\sin^{n}xdx}}$$ .</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Show that $$I_{2n + 2} \leqslant I_{2n + 1} \leqslant I_{2n}$$ .</item>
          <item>Use Exercise 56 to show that $$\frac{I_{2n + 2}}{I_{2n}} = \frac{2n + 1}{2n + 2}$$</item>
          <item>Use parts (a) and (b) to show that $$\frac{2n + 1}{2n + 2} \leqslant \frac{I_{2n + 1}}{I_{2n}} \leqslant 1$$ and deduce that $$\lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}I_{2n + 1} \slash I_{2n} = 1$$ .</item>
          <item>Use part (c) and Exercises 55 and 56 to show that $$\lim\limits_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{2}{1} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{7} \cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot \frac{2n}{2n - 1} \cdot \frac{2n}{2n + 1} = \frac{\pi}{2}$$ This formula is usually written as an infinite product: $$\frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{2}{1} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{3} \cdot \frac{4}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{5} \cdot \frac{6}{7} \cdot \cdot \cdot \cdot$$ and is called the Wallis product.</item>
          <item>We construct rectangles as follows. Start with a square of area $$1$$ and attach rectangles of area $$1$$ alternately beside or on top of the previous rectangle (see the figure). Find the limit of the ratios of width to height of these rectangles. Details Five-part illustration. Part 1: A square. Part 2: A rectangle that is divided into two equal parts. The first portion of the rectangle is shaded. Part 3: A rectangle, where a horizontal line is drawn at a quarter-point. The remaining portion of the rectangle is divided into two equal parts and shaded. Part 4: A rectangle, where a vertical line is drawn at the three-fourth point. A dotted horizontal line is also drawn at its quarter point until the vertical line. A dotted vertical line divides this portion equally. This area is shaded. Part 5: A rectangle, where a horizontal line is drawn near the quarter point. A vertical line is drawn at its three-fourth point below the horizontal line. A dotted horizontal line is drawn, which is divided into two by a dotted vertical line. The area below the first horizontal line is shaded.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
